%S A400939 1,3,6,10,5,11,18,9,18,28,14,7,20,34,17,33,50,25,44,22,43,65,88,112,
%T A400939 56,82,41,69,98,49,80,40,73,107,142,71,108,54,27,67,108,150,75,119,
%U A400939 164,210,105,153,202,101,152,76,38,19,74,37,94,47,106
%N A400939 The halving Recaman walk: a(1) = 1; for n > 1, a(n) = a(n-1)/2 if a(n-1) is even and a(n-1)/2 has not already appeared, otherwise a(n) = a(n-1) + n.
%C A400939 This is Recaman's sequence A005132 with "subtract n" replaced by "halve".
%C A400939 Conjecture: every positive integer appears.
%C A400939 The numbers 2, 12, 13, 51, 125 and 5091 (see A400940) first appear at n = 102, 333, 122524, 808054764, 28626066582 and 111647551484, each at the end of a long run of halvings. 6343 does not appear among the first 2*10^11 terms.
%C A400939 a(111647551484) = 5091. - _Benjamin Chaffin_, Oct 12 2026
%C A400939 Unlike Recaman's sequence A005132, this sequence seems to admit no shortcut for computing distant terms. In A005132 the visited numbers form long runs of consecutive integers, which allows whole blocks of terms to be skipped. In this sequence visited numbers come in runs of only about 1.68 consecutive integers on average (measured up to 10^10 terms), and roughly 45% of the steps are halvings whose outcome depends on which numbers were visited earlier, so the only known way to obtain a(n) is to compute all earlier terms. Whether a(n) can be computed in space or time polynomial in log n is an open question, impossibility in space would imply impossibility in time and PSPACE != EXP.
%C A400939 Conjecture: if 2 is replaced by any k that is not a power of 2, the resulting sequence eventually stops dividing and only adds n from then on. Checked by computer for all k <= 10^10. Between divisions the terms have the form A000217(n) + c, and the sequence stops dividing forever exactly when -c is not a residue of A000217(n) mod k. For k a power of 2 no such residues exist, so the sequence never stops dividing. For other k such residues exist, but it remains to be proven that the sequence eventually reaches one of them.
%H A400939 Zlatko Damijanic, <a href="/A400939/b400939.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400939 OEIS Wiki, <a href="https://oeis.org/wiki/A400K_Winners">A400K Winners</a>, 2026.
%H A400939 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/HalvingRecamanSequence.html">Halving Recamán Sequence</a>.
%t A400939 Module[{s, a = 1}, s[_] := False; s[a] = True; Join[{a}, Table[s[a = If[OddQ[a] || s[a/2], a + n, a/2]] = True; a, {n, 2, 100}]]] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 11 2026 *)
%o A400939 (Python)
%o A400939 def aupto(n):
%o A400939     s, p, out = {1}, 1, [1]
%o A400939     for i in range(2, n+1):
%o A400939         p = p//2 if p%2 == 0 and p//2 not in s else p+i
%o A400939         s.add(p); out.append(p)
%o A400939     return out
%Y A400939 Cf. A005132, A400940.
%K A400939 nonn,look,nice,changed
%O A400939 1,2
%A A400939 _Zlatko Damijanic_, Oct 10 2026
